Z||000007||Standard||12-15-1999||03:30 AM||||DXR_DAD - As far as I know the tach "floating" after you turn it off is normal.  When I turn my car off it floats up to around 1500.  Something to do with the way the gauge was designed.<P>Monkeyman - You won't see a distributor  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> Easiest way to tell is to look for the coils.  4 cyl will have 2 coils, 6 cyl will have 3, etc.  The plug wires run straight off of the coils, 1 coil for 2 cylinders.  The advantage is you never have to set the timing! Nothing mechanical to wear out.  ||||reg||1
Z||000008||Cliff Pennock||12-15-1999||04:04 AM||||This was actually one of the very first messages I ever wrote on this forum:<P> <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, lucidatypewriter">quote:</font><HR>That's because all (original) Fiero gauges are so called "air core meters". Normal gauges have a needle connected to a spring which forces them to a "0" position. The needle is attached to a coil around a magnet. When a current is applied to the coil, it causes the needle to move (the magnetic field generated by the coil attracts or repels the magnet). When the current disappears, the needle is pulled back to zero.<P>An air core meter uses two coils in an angle of 90 degrees. Each coil produces its own magnetic field resulting in a "magnetic vector". A magnet (at the base of the needle) placed in the middle will try to align its north- and southpole to this magnetic vector (like a compass) and will turn until it has done so.<P>The advantage over an ordinary gauge is that the needle is able to make full 360 degree turns and actually keep on turning. Also, you don't have to worry about the spring getting weaker over time, causing erratic readings.<P>When you turn off the ignition, the needle just stays where it is, because it has no spring attached to it forcing it to a zero position. Because the needle is quite hard to turn, it will not drop and point straight down.<P>The speedometer works exactly the same way but because most of the time you are standing still when you turn off the ignition, you don't notice it doesn't return...  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><BR><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><BR>||||reg||1
Z||000009||DXR_DAD||12-15-1999||09:38 AM||||Ok, Thanks Cliff that is some good info to know. I thought there was something wrong because with my 86SE I never see that happen. The tach always returns to zero. Are they different??||||reg||14
Z||000010||Standard||12-16-1999||03:31 AM||||I was looking all over for that info!  I knew I had seen it somewhere, but I couldn't remember where I had seen it..  I do remember reading it and thinking "wow, cool!"||||reg||14
